  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank. We know that the tank is being filled at 2 cubic feet per minute, except for the leakage caused by the hole. \n\nLet's first find the volume of the tank:\nVolume = π × r^2 × h = π × 4^2 × 10 = 160π cubic feet.\n\nWe have the tank filled to 5 feet after 10 minutes. So, the volume of water at that time is:\nVolume of water at 10 minutes = π × 4^2 × 5 = 80π cubic feet.\n\nNow, we need to find the leakage rate. Since the tank rises to 5 feet in 10 minutes, the volume it loses per minute is:\nVolume lost per minute = (80π - 200π) / 10 = 12π cubic feet per minute.\n\nThe leakage rate is proportional to the square root of the water's height. So, the leakage rate can be expressed as:\nLeakage rate = k × √(20 - t), where t is the time in minutes (0 ≤ t ≤ 10) and k is a constant.\n\nWe can find k by using the information that at 10 minutes, the leakage rate is 12π cubic feet per minute.\n12π = k × √(20 - 10)\nk = 24π\n\nSo, the leakage rate is:\nLeakage rate = 24π × √(20 - t)\n\nNow, we can find the net filling rate of water in the tank:\nNet filling rate = Filling rate - Leakage rate = 2 - (24π × √(20 - t))\n\nWe need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank:\nt = 0 to 10 minutes (because the tank is 10 feet tall)\n\nWe'll integrate the net filling rate from 0 to 10 to find the total volume of water in the tank after 10 minutes:\n∫(2 - 24π × √(20 - t)) dt from 0 to 10\n\nThis integral can be solved using substitution:\nLet u = 20 - t, du = -dt, thus dt = -du.\n∫(2 - 24π × √u) from 20 to 0\n\nNow, integrate:\n2t - 24π(u^(3/2)) from 20 to 0\n\nEvaluating the integral gives:\n(0 - 24π(20^(3/2))) - (2(10) - 24π(0^(3/2)))\n= -24π(20^(3/2)) - 20 + 24π(0)\n= -24π(20^(3/2)) - 20\n\nNow, we can find the time it takes to fill the entire tank:\nVolume = Net filling rate × Time\n80π = (80π - 200π + 20) × T\nT = 20 / (80π - 200π + 20)\nT = 20 / (-180π + 20)\nT ≈ 0.14 minutes\n\nSo, it will take approximately 0.14 minutes (or about 8.4 seconds) to fill the entire tank. However, this is after the tank has already reached a height of 5 feet, so the actual filling time will be less than 10 minutes. To find the exact time, we need to solve the original equation for the net filling rate and set it equal to 80π/π = 80 cubic feet per minute:\n\n80 = 2 - (24π × √(20 - t))\n\nSolving for t gives t ≈ 5.55555556 minutes.\n\nTherefore, it will take approximately 5.56 minutes (or about 354 seconds) to fill the entire tank.",
